import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The database",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's database, Postgres, or SQLite or Turso in the server's process, as the app's program says: queries whose rows decode into the record you annotate, transactions that take jobs with them, migrations built into the app, notifications, and roc-pg, a client in Roc whose queries are checked when the app is built.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "choose", label: "Postgres or SQLite" },
		{ id: "turso", label: "Turso" },
		{ id: "query", label: "A query" },
		{ id: "rows", label: "Rows, as records" },
		{ id: "write", label: "Writing" },
		{ id: "transactions", label: "Transactions" },
		{ id: "migrations", label: "Migrations" },
		{ id: "notify", label: "Notifications" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where it can be used" },
		{ id: "roc-pg", label: "roc-pg: queries checked at build" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · The database"
				title="One database, and the types you say"
			>
				The server platform is opinionated: an app has one database, Postgres or SQLite (on SQLite's own
				engine, or on Turso's), as its program says. <code>Db</code> runs a statement with its parameters and gives back the rows, decoded into the
				record the caller annotated; a transaction keeps one connection for a call; migrations run once
				each, when the server starts. The jobs queue and the actors' state live there too.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="choose">Postgres or SQLite</Heading>
			<p>
				An app picks its database when it is made, in its program, and writes its SQL for it: nothing is
				translated, and an app runs on one. <code>Postgres</code> is the default: it is at
				<code>--database-url</code> (or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>), and without one the app has no database.
				<code>Sqlite(path)</code> is a file in the server's own process, at <code>path</code> from where the
				server starts, made (with its directory) when it is missing, so the app runs with nothing set and
				nothing beside it.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tcontext: |_started| {},\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\tdatabase: Sqlite(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>--database-url sqlite:/var/lib/plate.db</code> puts a SQLite app's file elsewhere (on a volume, a
				test's own); a URL of the other kind stops the server as it starts, saying which the app wants.
				SQLite suits an app on one server (the platform runs one), with no database to run beside it:
				<code>examples/plate</code> is one. Postgres suits data other programs share, or more than a server's
				disk holds.
			</p>
			<p>
				A package says which it works with, in its plugin's needs: <code>Database</code> (either),
				<code>Postgres</code> or <code>Sqlite</code>; one that needs the other stops the server as it starts,
				saying so. A package with SQL for both asks <code>Db.backend!</code> which to run, and lists a migration
				for each (<code>{"{ name: \"0001_jobs\", sql: lite, on: Sqlite }"}</code>): the jobs plugin does both.
			</p>

			<Heading id="turso">Turso: SQLite's SQL on another engine</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Turso(path)</code> is <code>Sqlite(path)</code> with another engine under it:
				<a href="https://github.com/tursodatabase/turso">Turso</a>, SQLite written again in Rust, in the
				server's process. Its SQL is SQLite's and its file is in SQLite's format, so what this page says of
				SQLite holds for it: the app's SQL, and a package's, a plugin's migrations and needs for SQLite.
				<code>Db.backend!</code> answers <code>Sqlite</code> on both; <code>Db.engine!</code> tells them apart.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tcontext: |_started| {},\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\tdatabase: Turso(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				The URL picks the engine: <code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db</code> runs a SQLite app on Turso,
				and <code>sqlite:</code> a Turso app on SQLite, which is how to try one against the other.
			</p>
			<p>
				What Turso adds is writes at once. Its file is in MVCC mode: a transaction (<code>Db.transaction!</code>)
				begins <code>concurrent</code>, on a snapshot of its own, and writes beside the others; when two write
				the same row, the one that commits second rolls back and its body runs again (up to a hundred times),
				so keep what isn't the database's (a fetch, an email) out of a transaction. A write outside one (an
				insert, an update) is its own concurrent transaction, tried again the same way; a schema change (a
				migration) takes the file to itself. Two transactions that read rows and write different ones both
				commit, where on SQLite the second would wait and see the first's.
			</p>
			<p>
				Some things differ. In MVCC mode the file is Turso's alone: SQLite says so, and
				<code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db?mvcc=off</code>, once, gives it back (that mode also keeps
				Turso's writes taking turns, as SQLite's do). The server keeps a Turso file to itself while it runs (an
				OS lock on it), so <code>sqlite3</code>, or a test's <code>node:sqlite</code>, opens it once the server
				has stopped. Turso has no FTS5, so <code>examples/notes</code>' search and <code>examples/plate</code>'s need
				SQLite, and no <code>order by</code> inside an aggregate (<code>json_group_array(value order by key)</code>,
				which <code>examples/board</code> uses). And Turso is younger
				than SQLite (0.8, before its 1.0): once it has checkpointed rows, deleting many of them slows what
				follows badly until its next checkpoint (<code>pragma wal_checkpoint(truncate)</code> after such a
				delete avoids it; <code>?mvcc=off</code> doesn't have it).
			</p>
			<p>
				How they compare: <a href={Paths.examples_sqlite_vs_turso}>examples/sqlite-vs-turso</a> times the same
				work on both, in Roc, in the server (writes, reads, scans, JSON, and eight callers at once), and its
				page has the numbers.
			</p>

			<Heading id="query">A query</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.all!</code> runs a statement with its parameters and answers its rows. The type says what a
				row is; the platform's tests' app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_notes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_notes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A parameter is <code>Null</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>Int</code> or <code>Bool</code>, named
				<code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>… in both. Postgres is sent each as text, which it reads as the type its
				place wants (<code>$1</code> in <code>where id = $1</code> is a <code>bigint</code>); SQLite is given each
				as it is (a <code>Bool</code> as 1 or 0), and a column of numbers takes text that is one. A statement is
				never put together from strings: its values go as parameters.
			</p>
			<p>
				A list is one parameter with <code>Db.array</code>, on Postgres: an array, written as Postgres writes one in
				text (<code>{"{\"work\",\"to do\",NULL}"}</code>), which it reads as the array its place wants, of text or
				of numbers: <code>= any($1)</code>, <code>{"@> $1"}</code>, <code>$1::bigint[]</code>,
				<code>unnest($1::text[])</code> (alone, <code>unnest($1)</code> can't tell which array, so it takes the
				cast). Each text is quoted, so a comma, a brace or the word NULL in one stays text. SQLite has no
				arrays: a list is JSON text there, read with <code>json_each</code>
				(<code>where tag in (select value from json_each($1))</code>).
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An array as a parameter"
				source={"tagged : Try(List({ id : I64 }), [DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str)])\ntagged = Db.all!(\"select id from notes where tags @> $1\", [Db.array(tags.map(|tag| Text(tag)))])\n\nDb.run!(\"delete from notes where id = any($1)\", [Db.array(ids.map(|id| Int(id)))])"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>

			<Heading id="rows">Rows, as records</Heading>
			<p>
				The host reads each row's columns, as Postgres typed them, and hands Roc the rows as JSON;
				<code>Json.parse</code> decodes each into the record the caller annotated, by column name. A row
				that doesn't fit is <code>BadRow</code>, saying why.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nulls are typed.</strong> A column that may be null is read as <code>Db.Nullable(a)</code>:
					<code>Null</code> or <code>Some(a)</code>, matched like any tag union. The record says what may be
					null, not the column's name or the table; a null in a column the record reads as a plain value is
					<code>BadRow</code>, which names the column.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What is read, from Postgres:</strong> booleans, integers, floats, text, <code>json</code> and
					<code>jsonb</code>, <code>uuid</code>, timestamps and dates (as text). Anything else asks for a cast
					(<code>::text</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From SQLite:</strong> each value as what it is (an integer or a real a number, text a
					string), a column declared <code>boolean</code> as a <code>Bool</code> and one declared
					<code>json</code> as its JSON. A boolean worked out in a statement (<code>exists (…)</code>) is 0 or 1,
					an <code>I64</code>. SQLite has no time of its own: milliseconds in an integer are the advice.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="write">Writing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.run!</code> runs a statement and answers how many rows it changed; <code>Db.script!</code> runs
				statements with no parameters, one after another, in one transaction (or in the call's, if it has
				one open).
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_insert.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_insert.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="transactions">Transactions</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.transaction!</code> keeps one connection for the call: what its body writes, and the jobs it
				queues, stay only if it returns <code>Ok</code>. An <code>Err</code>, a failing statement or a crash
				rolls it all back, and a call that ends with a transaction still open has it rolled back.
			</p>
			<p>
				On SQLite writes take turns, and reads go on beside them: a transaction holds the write lock from
				its start, so every other write waits until it ends. Keep anything slow (a request to another
				server, a wait) out of one; on Postgres it would hold only the rows it wrote.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_transaction.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_transaction.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The body can <code>?</code> what <code>Db</code>, <code>Job</code> and the app itself return, with
				nothing mapped from one to another: their errors are open unions (<code>Db.run!</code>'s is
				<code>[DbErr(Str), ..others]</code>, <code>Job.enqueue!</code>'s <code>[JobErr(Str), ..others]</code>),
				so the transaction's error is every one of them (with an app's own <code>Err(Refused)</code>,
				<code>[DbErr(Str), JobErr(Str), Refused]</code>), and the caller matches each case.
			</p>
			<p>
				A job queued in a transaction is a row in it, so it runs only if the transaction commits, and the
				workers are woken when it does (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="migrations">Migrations</Heading>
			<p>
				An app's migrations are in its executable: <code>program.migrations</code> lists them, each a name and
				its SQL, which Roc reads from a <code>.sql</code> file when the app is compiled. When the server
				starts, the platform's Roc applies them in the order listed, each once, each in a transaction,
				recorded by name in <code>rocx_migrations</code>; two starting at once (a deploy's old server and its
				new one) take turns (on Postgres an advisory lock, on SQLite its write lock). The platform's own tables (<code>rocx_actors</code>) come
				first, then the plugins' (the jobs plugin's <code>rocx_jobs</code>, recorded as <code>jobs/0001_jobs</code>),
				then the app's. One that fails stops the server, saying why.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_migrations.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_migrations.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.platform_migration.file} source={Sources.platform_migration.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain={Bool.True} />

			<Heading id="notify">Notifications</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.notify!(channel, payload)</code> tells whoever listens to <code>channel</code>
				(<code>Db.listen!</code>, then <code>Live.next!</code>): when the call's transaction commits, never if it
				rolls back, or at once outside one. On Postgres it is <code>pg_notify</code>, which another process
				that writes to the database can send too; on SQLite it is the server's own. It is how the job workers
				sleep until a job is queued.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where it can be used</Heading>
			<p>
				Anywhere only the server runs: an endpoint, a middleware, a job, an actor, a stream. A component is
				compiled for the browser too, so it reads its data from a database through a GET endpoint
				(<code>Endpoint.get!</code>, answered in-process as the server renders the page): the handler runs
				only on the server, so it may query, and the browser's copy has only the endpoint's method and URL
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#user"}>who is asking</a>). A
				statement takes a connection from a pool (16 to Postgres, 8 to SQLite's or Turso's file), and gives
				it back; a transaction keeps one until it ends. Statements are prepared once per connection, and
				kept. SQLite's and Turso's run on threads of their own, so a call waiting on one holds none of
				Roc's.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>just db</code> runs a Postgres in Docker and prints its URL; the server takes it as
				<code>--database-url</code> or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>. With none, a query answers that there is no
				database, and the server's other parts work. Connections use TLS when the database offers it (or the
				URL's <code>sslmode</code> asks for it), checked against the web's certificate authorities. SQLite's
				file is in WAL mode, with its <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code> beside it; a backup is
				<code>sqlite3 data/plate.db ".backup copy.db"</code>, or the file with the server stopped (Turso's
				only so: the server holds it while it runs).
			</p>

			<Heading id="roc-pg">roc-pg: queries checked at build</Heading>
			<p>
				For Postgres, <a href="https://github.com/niclas-ahden/roc-pg">roc-pg</a> is a client written in Roc. It
				speaks Postgres's protocol over a socket the platform gives it (<code>Tcp</code>), logs in with the
				platform's <code>Random</code>, and checks each query against a schema when the app is compiled: a
				column that isn't there, a parameter of the wrong type, a column that may be null read as one that
				can't, each fails the build. The platform's example (<code>server/examples/roc-pg</code>) uses the
				same <code>schema.sql</code> as its migration, so what roc-pg checks against is what the server
				applied.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_rocpg.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_rocpg.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Use either, or both. <code>Db</code> has a pool, transactions that take jobs with them, and rows
				decoded into the record you annotate; roc-pg has queries the compiler checks, and a connection per
				use (it has no pool). Checking the example takes under a second from cold, and an optimized build
				about six; the platform's tests build it and run it against Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Db</code>'s queries aren't checked at build:</strong> a row is decoded by the record
					each caller annotates, and a mistake shows when the query runs. roc-pg's are checked.
				</li>
				<li><strong>Parameters</strong> are four kinds and arrays of them; bytes aren't yet, and a row reads an array column through a cast (<code>array_to_json(tags)</code>).</li>
				<li><strong>No cursors or streaming:</strong> a query's rows come back whole.</li>
				<li><strong>SQLite</strong> is a file: no database in memory alone (the server's connections couldn't share it), and a blob is read through <code>hex(…)</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>Turso</strong>'s sync to Turso's cloud and its own full-text search aren't used yet; in MVCC mode it has no <code>WITHOUT ROWID</code> tables and no <code>REINDEX</code>.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
